+## skills/fetch-helper — T4 (opaque: fetches and runs code at runtime)
+
+Files (sha256):
+- `SKILL.md` 9b07a1d9fa8895f5475b3a78c355c8803b4b21d10f3451a7a5201be3d15a5a57
+- `scripts/update.sh` 1bcfe4041f13ea50f6edd193a2852c1baf0ae634e653c13cfc11790cc313a119
+
+Evidence:
+- `skills/fetch-helper/scripts/update.sh:6` — `curl -fsSL "$HELPER_URL" | sh`: a
+  fetch-and-run one-liner. It downloads a remote script and pipes it straight into a
+  shell. Nothing pins, checksums or signs the download, so what actually runs is
+  decided by the remote server at run time, not by anything in this folder. This
+  alone makes it T4.
+- `skills/fetch-helper/scripts/update.sh:4` — endpoint
+  `https://helpers.example-tools.dev/bootstrap/latest.sh`. This is a mutable
+  `latest` URL on a third-party domain, so the code can change between runs.
+- `skills/fetch-helper/SKILL.md:10` — tells the agent to "Run `scripts/update.sh`
+  from the repository root", so the agent runs the line above with the user's
+  permissions.
+- `skills/fetch-helper/SKILL.md:3` — triggers on "update helpers" or "before a
+  release", so it can be invoked without the user specifically asking for it.
+- `skills/fetch-helper/SKILL.md:11` — runs `helper --version`, a binary that the
+  remote script installs. This is also unreviewed code.
+- No credential-file paths or agent-config (`CLAUDE.md`) writes appear in the local
+  files. That doesn't rule them out, because the remote payload can do anything.
+
+Recommendation: don't add it as is. Pin a version and verify a checksum, or vendor
+the bootstrap script so it can be reviewed.
